(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/doc_type "adr")
  (:adr/id "adr-2609110900-the-coercion-fixture-omitted-the-coerced-field")
  (:adr/topic "q9-generator-coercion-fixture")
  (:adr/title "The coercion fixture was built from :required, so it omitted the coerced field")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-10")
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"))
  (:adr/repos (vector "com-junkawasaki/root" "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang"))
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609110800-a-generator-fallback-produced-a-selector-that-measured-nothing"))
  (:adr/body
  "## Context

The plan for this iteration was to regenerate `com-abb-robotics` with the
ref-arity-aware generator, the way `com-accela` was regenerated the iteration
before. Diffing the candidate against what ships stopped that, and the reason is
the finding.

## What the diff showed

    shipped    (= sel 3) \"{\\\"model\\\":\\\"IRB-6700\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"idle\\\",\\\"payloadKg\\\":\\\"150.5\\\"}\"
    generated  (= sel 3) \"{\\\"model\\\":\\\"v\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"v\\\"}\"

Selector 3 is the coercion entity's document. It was built from `(:required
float-ent)`, and Robot coerces `payloadKg` to `:float` while requiring only
`[model status]`. So the generated fixture omits the one field the arm exists to
exercise. The hand-written component carried it because a person noticed.

**Regenerating would have been a regression, and the regression would have been
invisible**: the file compiles, `amu check` answers `:ok true`, and the
validation oracle that consumes selector 3 keeps returning numbers. Nothing
downstream says that the float field stopped being present.

## It is the common case, not the rare one

Reproduce by reading each oracle's `entity-specs` and asking which coerced
fields are absent from `:required`:

    com-aadhaar        none
    com-aave           ReserveData, EModeCategory, ExecuteBorrowParams
    com-abb-robotics   Robot:payloadKg
    com-accela         none (declares no coercion at all)
    com-adobe          Frame:height
    com-acquia         Asset, Channel, ContentEntry, Experiment

Four of six sampled pilots declare a coercion on a field they do not require.

## Decision

The fixture is `:required` plus the coerced fields, each given a value of its
kind -- `7` for int, `1.5` for float, `true` for bool.

## The control

A fix that added a coerced field unconditionally would also make the symptom go
away, and would put a field in the fixture of a table that has none. So the
check is the pair:

    com-abb-robotics  gains \"payloadKg\":\"1.5\"
    com-accela        gains nothing -- it declares no coercion anywhere

Both regenerated components pass `amu check :ok true`, and regenerating
`com-accela`'s shipped component is byte-identical, so the generator is
idempotent.

## com-abb-robotics is still not regenerated

Its component is hand-written, its fixture values are domain-specific rather
than placeholders, and its migration record quotes measured hash values that a
regeneration would move. With this fix the generated form is structurally
right, so regeneration is now a reasonable step -- but it is a step that must
re-measure and rewrite those recorded values, and that deserves its own run
rather than riding along with a generator change.

## A stale ref cost me a wrong conclusion, in the middle of this

Checking idempotency, I diffed a regenerated `com-accela` against `origin/main`
and got 39 differing lines, which reads as a generator that is not idempotent.
It was not. I had fetched `com-abb-robotics` and `kotoba-lang` this iteration
and not `com-accela`, so the local `origin/main` ref still pointed at the commit
before last iteration's regeneration. GitHub's `main` was correct throughout.

This is the workspace's own standing warning -- checkout, west pin and the
repository's `main` are three different things -- reached through the ref rather
than the working tree. After fetching, the diff is zero. The lesson that
generalises: a comparison against `origin/main` is only as current as the last
fetch of THAT repository, and fetching a sibling does not count.")))
